{
  "ID": "2.1",
  "Context/Background": "ASXL1 plays key roles in epigenetic regulation of early developmental gene expression. De novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1 cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a rare neurodevelopmental condition characterized by severe intellectual disabilities, distinctive facial features, hypertrichosis, increased risk of Wilms tumor, and variable congenital anomalies, including heart defects and severe skeletal defects giving rise to a typical BOS posture. These BOS-causing ASXL1 variants are also high-prevalence somatic driver mutations in acute myeloid leukemia.  We used primary cells from individuals with BOS (n = 18) and controls (n = 49) to dissect gene regulatory changes caused by ASXL1 mutations using comprehensive multiomics assays for chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq), DNA methylation, histone methylation binding, and transcriptome in peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts.\n",
  "Prompt": "\nBased on the results of the blood and fibroblast transcriptomic DEG analyses of BOS vs controls, provide a discussion of the common and distinct pathways found between blood and fibroblast and any relevance to the presence in the BOS samples of ASXL1 truncating mutations.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-15 16:55:18",
  "elapsed_seconds": 178.76,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nBohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS) is driven by de-novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1, a core epigenetic regulator of developmental gene expression. In this study, peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts from BOS patients (n = 18) were compared to controls (n = 49) by RNA-seq and differential expression analysis. Top DEGs in fibroblasts include developmental adhesion molecules (CNTN4/6), neuronal-related genes (STMN2, GNAO1), planar-cell-polarity regulator VANGL2, heat-shock family member HSPA12B, and glycosyltransferase UGT3A2. In blood, prominent DEGs include epigenetic/chromatin factors (CBX2, MYT1L), immune/hematopoietic markers (DNTT, CFH, TNFSF18), neuronal regulators (GRIK5, ASIC1), and ribosomal protein RPL3L.  \n\nKeywords  \n• planar cell polarity / Wnt signaling  \n• neuronal differentiation & adhesion  \n• epigenetic/chromatin regulation  \n• immune/hematopoietic function  \n\nCandidate Genes  \nVANGL2, GRIK5, CBX2, CNTN4, DNTT, MYT1L, HSPA12B, CFH  \n\nCommon Pathways  \n1. Planar cell polarity/Wnt signaling (VANGL2 up in both)  \n2. Neuronal‐related signaling and adhesion (GRIK5, contactins)  \n3. Ion channel activity (GRIK5, ASIC1)  \n\nDistinct Fibroblast Pathways  \n• ECM/adhesion remodeling (TNMD, EMID1)  \n• Metabolic/glycosylation processes (UGT3A2)  \n• Stress response/heat-shock (HSPA12B, MAP3K9)  \n\nDistinct Blood Pathways  \n• Polycomb/epigenetic machinery (CBX2, MYT1L)  \n• Immune and hematopoietic differentiation (DNTT, CFH, TNFSF18)  \n• Protein synthesis/ribosome (RPL3L)  \n\nAnalysis  \n• Both tissues show upregulation of VANGL2 and GRIK5, implicating dysregulated PCP/Wnt and excitatory signaling as a shared consequence of ASXL1 loss-of-function.  \n• Fibroblast-specific DEGs highlight extracellular matrix remodeling and stress response—possibly reflecting tissue-restricted chromatin changes at lineage-specific enhancers.  \n• Blood-specific DEGs center on chromatin regulators and immune/hematopoietic-lineage genes, consistent with ASXL1’s role in myeloid differentiation and its frequent somatic truncations in AML.  \n• The common signature of developmental-neuronal genes alongside tissue-specific modules underscores how ASXL1 truncations perturb both core developmental programs and cell-type-specific gene networks.  \n\nImplications  \nASXL1 truncating variants likely disrupt H3K27me3-mediated repression at developmental and lineage-defining loci, driving aberrant activation of neuronal/PCP pathways in both mesenchymal and hematopoietic compartments while also unleashing tissue-specific programs (ECM/stress in fibroblasts; epigenetic regulators and immune effectors in blood). This dual pattern may underlie both the multi-system congenital anomalies in BOS and the predisposition to hematologic malignancies.  \n\nNext Steps & Clarification  \n1. Perform pathway enrichment (e.g., GSEA) across the full DEG lists to quantify these modules and confirm statistical significance of PCP/Wnt and chromatin-regulator signatures.  \n2. Validate candidate overlapping DEGs (VANGL2, GRIK5) at the protein level in both tissues to confirm functional dysregulation.  \n3. Map H3K27me3 changes by CUT&RUN/ChIP-seq at VANGL2 and contactin enhancers to link ASXL1 loss directly to their misexpression.  \n4. Evaluate whether immune pathway activation in BOS blood parallels features of clonal hematopoiesis observed in ASXL1-mutant AML.",
